window.CHESS_GUIDE_MD = {"en": "# ♟️ My Ihsan Chess Guide\n\nA step-by-step guide for players of **Explore › 3D chess** in My Ihsan. Read the chapters in order, or go straight to the one you need.\n\n| # | Chapter | What you will learn |\n|---|---------|---------------------|\n| 1 | [Fastest checkmates and opening traps](#chapter-1-fastest-checkmates-and-opening-traps) | Fool's mate, Scholar's mate, Légal's mate and other quick wins, and how to stop each one |\n| 2 | [Popular openings](#chapter-2-popular-openings) | The openings strong players use most, with their first moves and the idea behind them |\n| 3 | [Thinking like a professional](#chapter-3-thinking-like-a-professional) | How masters choose a move, read notation, check for blunders and study games |\n| 4 | [Strategy and tactics](#chapter-4-strategy-and-tactics) | Opening principles, middlegame plans, tactical patterns, mating patterns and endgames |\n| 5 | [Training plan in My Ihsan](#chapter-5-training-plan-in-my-ihsan) | A 4-week plan using the robot levels, clocks, undo and the move list |\n\nEvery move line in this guide has been played through the My Ihsan chess engine to make sure it is legal and that each checkmate really is checkmate. Tap **▶ Play on board** under any line to step through it move by move.\n\n## How to read the moves\n\nThe move list in the game (📜 Moves) uses **standard algebraic notation**, and so does this guide.\n\n- Squares are named by **file** (a–h, left to right from white's side) and **rank** (1–8, from white's side). White's king starts on **e1**, black's on **e8**.\n- Pieces: **K** king, **Q** queen, **R** rook, **B** bishop, **N** knight. Pawns have no letter: `e4` means a pawn moves to e4.\n- `x` = capture (`Bxf7` = bishop takes on f7), `+` = check, `#` = checkmate.\n- `O-O` = castle kingside, `O-O-O` = castle queenside.\n- `1.e4 e5` = white's first move, then black's reply. `3...Nf6` = black's third move.\n- `?` = a mistake, `??` = a blunder, `!` = a good move.\n\n## Piece names in the game\n\n| Symbol | English | Classic theme (BM) | Malay court theme |\n|:-:|---|---|---|\n| ♚ K | King | Raja | Sultan |\n| ♛ Q | Queen | Menteri | Permaisuri |\n| ♜ R | Rook | Tir | Laksamana |\n| ♝ B | Bishop | Gajah | Bendahara |\n| ♞ N | Knight | Kuda | Hulubalang |\n| ♟ P | Pawn | Bidak | Pesilat |\n\n| English | Bahasa Melayu |\n|---|---|\n| Check | Sah |\n| Checkmate | Mat |\n| Stalemate | Buntu |\n| Draw | Seri |\n| Castling | Istana |\n| Promotion | Naik pangkat |\n\n## A little history\n\nChess reached the Muslim world as *shatranj* from Persia, and for several centuries the strongest players and writers on the game lived in the Abbasid lands. **Al-Adli ar-Rumi** (9th century) wrote one of the earliest known books on chess, and **as-Suli** (died about 946) was the champion of his time in Baghdad; his endgame studies are still discussed today. The \"Arabian mate\" in chapter 4 is named after this tradition.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 1: Fastest checkmates and opening traps\n\nThese are the quickest ways a game can end. Learn them for two reasons:\n\n1. **So they never happen to you.** Most of these only work if the other side makes a bad move.\n2. **To punish beginners.** Against the robot on level 1, or a friend who is new, they can win in a few moves.\n\n> ⚠️ A good opponent will not fall for them. If you play only for quick traps, you will often end up with a worse position. Use them as a bonus, and play the sound openings from [chapter 2](#chapter-2-popular-openings) as your main plan.\n\nThe weak point in every one of these traps is the same: the squares next to the king that only the king protects, especially **f7** (for black) and **f2** (for white) at the start of the game.\n\n---\n\n### Fool's mate: 2 moves (the fastest possible)\n\n```\n1.f3 e5\n2.g4 Qh4#\n```\n\nWhite opens the diagonal from h4 to e1 by moving the f- and g-pawns, and black's queen mates at once. The same thing happens after `1.f4 e5 2.g4 Qh4#`.\n\nWhite can fall into the same idea too: `1.e4 g5 2.d4 f6 3.Qh5#`.\n\n**Lesson:** at the start, do not move the f- and g-pawns in front of your king without a reason.\n\n---\n\n### Scholar's mate: 4 moves\n\n```\n1.e4 e5\n2.Bc4 Nc6\n3.Qh5 Nf6??\n4.Qxf7#\n```\n\nThe white queen and bishop both aim at f7. Black only protected e5 and forgot f7.\n\nA version with the queen on f3: `1.e4 e5 2.Qf3 Nc6 3.Bc4 d6?? 4.Qxf7#`.\n\n#### How to stop Scholar's mate\n\nAfter `3.Qh5`, black should protect e5 **and** block the queen:\n\n```\n1.e4 e5\n2.Bc4 Nc6\n3.Qh5 g6!\n4.Qf3 Nf6!\n5.Qb3 Qe7\n6.Nc3 Nd4\n```\n\nNow the white queen has been pushed around, black has developed with tempo (each move made a threat) and black is better. **Bringing the queen out early is a weakness when the opponent knows the defence.**\n\nThe simplest rule: after white's bishop goes to c4, play `...Nf6` early so the knight guards h5 and the queen cannot come there.\n\n---\n\n### Légal's mate: 7 moves\n\nNamed after the 18th-century French master Légal. It shows a famous idea: **giving up the queen to deliver mate with minor pieces.**\n\n```\n1.e4 e5\n2.Nf3 d6\n3.Bc4 Bg4\n4.Nc3 g6?\n5.Nxe5! Bxd1??\n6.Bxf7+ Ke7\n7.Nd5#\n```\n\nBlack thinks the knight on f3 is pinned to the queen and wins the queen, but white's bishop and two knights mate the king. Black should have played `5...dxe5`, which only loses a pawn (after `6.Qxg4`).\n\n**Lesson:** a pin on the queen is not a real pin. The piece *can* move if the reward is bigger than the queen.\n\n---\n\n### Blackburne Shilling trap: 7 moves (black wins)\n\nNamed after the English master Joseph Blackburne, who reportedly won small bets with it. (In My Ihsan we only learn the pattern, never any betting.)\n\n```\n1.e4 e5\n2.Nf3 Nc6\n3.Bc4 Nd4\n4.Nxe5? Qg5!\n5.Nxf7?? Qxg2\n6.Rf1 Qxe4+\n7.Be2 Nf3#\n```\n\n`3...Nd4` is not a good move, but it sets a trap. White grabs the pawn, then the rook on f7, and gets mated by a **smothered mate**: the king is boxed in by its own pieces.\n\n**How white avoids it:** answer `3...Nd4` with `4.Nxd4 exd4 5.c3` or simply `4.O-O`. Do not take the e5 pawn.\n\n---\n\n### Englund Gambit trap: 8 moves (black wins)\n\n```\n1.d4 e5\n2.dxe5 Nc6\n3.Nf3 Qe7\n4.Bf4? Qb4+\n5.Bd2 Qxb2\n6.Bc3? Bb4\n7.Qd2 Bxc3\n8.Qxc3 Qc1#\n```\n\nBlack's queen grabs the b2 pawn and white's back rank collapses. White should play `4.Qd5` or `4.Nc3` instead of `4.Bf4`.\n\n---\n\n### Smothered mate in the Caro-Kann: 6 moves\n\n```\n1.e4 c6\n2.d4 d5\n3.Nc3 dxe4\n4.Nxe4 Nd7\n5.Qe2 Ngf6??\n6.Nd6#\n```\n\nThe queen on e2 pins the e7 pawn to the king, so `Nd6` cannot be taken. The black king is surrounded by its own pieces. Black should play `5...Ndf6` instead, so the d7 knight leaves and the king gets a square.\n\n**Lesson:** when your opponent's queen stands on the same line as your king, be careful about blocking your king in.\n\n---\n\n### Kieninger trap (Budapest Gambit): 8 moves (black wins)\n\n```\n1.d4 Nf6\n2.c4 e5\n3.dxe5 Ng4\n4.Bf4 Nc6\n5.Nf3 Bb4+\n6.Nbd2 Qe7\n7.a3 Ngxe5\n8.axb4?? Nd3#\n```\n\nTaking the bishop opens a path for the smothered mate on d3: the white king is blocked by its own queen and knight. White should play `8.Nxe5 Nxe5 9.e3` and stay safe.\n\n---\n\n### Fried Liver Attack (not a mate, but a strong attack)\n\nA favourite of club players. It starts like this:\n\n```\n1.e4 e5\n2.Nf3 Nc6\n3.Bc4 Nf6\n4.Ng5 d5\n5.exd5 Nxd5?\n6.Nxf7! Kxf7\n7.Qf3+ Ke6\n8.Nc3\n```\n\nThe black king is pulled into the middle of the board and white attacks. **Black's correct defence** is to not recapture with the knight:\n\n```\n5...Na5!\n6.Bb5+ c6\n7.dxc6 bxc6\n8.Be2 h6\n```\n\nBlack has given a pawn, but chases the white pieces and gets fast development.\n\n---\n\n### Traps that win the queen\n\n#### Petrov trap\n\n```\n1.e4 e5\n2.Nf3 Nf6\n3.Nxe5 Nxe4?\n4.Qe2 Nf6??\n5.Nc6+\n```\n\nThe knight moves with a **discovered check** from the queen on e2 and also attacks black's queen on d8. Black loses the queen. (After `3.Nxe5` black should play `3...d6` first, then take on e4.)\n\n#### Elephant trap (Queen's Gambit Declined)\n\n```\n1.d4 d5\n2.c4 e6\n3.Nc3 Nf6\n4.Bg5 Nbd7\n5.cxd5 exd5\n6.Nxd5? Nxd5!\n7.Bxd8 Bb4+\n8.Qd2 Bxd2+\n9.Kxd2 Kxd8\n```\n\nWhite thinks the knight on f6 is pinned and grabs a pawn. Black gives up the queen, wins it back with check, and ends up a **whole piece ahead**.\n\n---\n\n### Summary: how to never lose to a quick trap\n\n1. **Protect f7 / f2** in the first moves. Watch any queen or bishop aiming there.\n2. **Don't move the f- and g-pawns** in front of your king early.\n3. **Develop knights and bishops before the queen.**\n4. **Castle early** to move the king away from the centre.\n5. **Before you grab a \"free\" pawn or piece, ask: why is it free?**\n6. **A pin on the queen can be broken** if the reward is checkmate or more material.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 2: Popular openings\n\nYou do not need to memorise long lines. Strong players remember the **idea** of an opening and the first few moves. If your opponent leaves the book, you still know what you are trying to do.\n\n**Start with one opening for white and two replies for black** (one against `1.e4`, one against `1.d4`). Play them again and again against the robot.\n\n| If you like... | Play with white | Play with black vs 1.e4 | Play with black vs 1.d4 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Simple, classical play | Italian Game | 1...e5 (Italian / Ruy Lopez) | Queen's Gambit Declined |\n| A system with few lines to learn | London System | Caro-Kann | Slav Defence |\n| Sharp, attacking games | Scotch Game | Sicilian Defence | King's Indian Defence |\n\n---\n\n### Openings with 1.e4\n\n#### Italian Game (Giuoco Piano): best first opening\n\n```\n1.e4 e5\n2.Nf3 Nc6\n3.Bc4 Bc5\n4.c3 Nf6\n5.d3 d6\n6.O-O O-O\n```\n\n**Idea for white:** the bishop on c4 aims at f7, `c3` prepares `d4` to take the centre, and the slow `d3` keeps everything safe. Later white often plays `Re1`, `Nbd2-f1-g3` and pushes `d4` or `b4`.\n\n**Idea for black:** copy the same healthy setup, castle, and look for `...d5` or `...a6` with `...Ba7`.\n\nA related line is the **Two Knights Defence** (`3...Nf6`). Against `4.d3 Be7 5.O-O O-O` you get a calm game. If white plays `4.Ng5`, see the Fried Liver in [chapter 1](#chapter-1-fastest-checkmates-and-opening-traps).\n\n#### Ruy Lopez (Spanish Opening): the opening of world champions\n\n```\n1.e4 e5\n2.Nf3 Nc6\n3.Bb5 a6\n4.Ba4 Nf6\n5.O-O Be7\n6.Re1 b5\n7.Bb3 d6\n8.c3 O-O\n```\n\n**Idea:** the bishop on b5 attacks the knight that defends e5. White builds slowly with `c3` and `d4`, and the game is a long fight about the centre. It is rich and deep, so it is a good second opening once the Italian feels comfortable.\n\n#### Scotch Game: open the centre at once\n\n```\n1.e4 e5\n2.Nf3 Nc6\n3.d4 exd4\n4.Nxd4 Nf6\n5.Nxc6 bxc6\n6.e5 Qe7\n```\n\n**Idea:** white opens lines immediately and gets active pieces. Good for players who like tactics.\n\n#### Sicilian Defence: black's most popular fighting reply\n\nThe **Najdorf** (favourite of Fischer and Kasparov):\n\n```\n1.e4 c5\n2.Nf3 d6\n3.d4 cxd4\n4.Nxd4 Nf6\n5.Nc3 a6\n```\n\nThe **Dragon** (the g7 bishop looks like a dragon's breath along the diagonal):\n\n```\n1.e4 c5\n2.Nf3 d6\n3.d4 cxd4\n4.Nxd4 Nf6\n5.Nc3 g6\n6.Be3 Bg7\n7.f3 O-O\n8.Qd2 Nc6\n```\n\n**Idea:** black trades the c-pawn for white's d-pawn, so the position is unbalanced. Black often attacks on the queenside with the half-open c-file while white attacks the king. Games are sharp, so know the ideas before you play it.\n\nIf you are white and do not want to learn the big Sicilian lines, try the **Alapin**: `1.e4 c5 2.c3 Nf6 3.e5 Nd5 4.d4 cxd4 5.Nf3 Nc6`.\n\n#### French Defence: solid and counter-attacking\n\n```\n1.e4 e6\n2.d4 d5\n3.Nc3 Nf6\n4.Bg5 Be7\n5.e5 Nfd7\n```\n\nThe **Advance variation**: `1.e4 e6 2.d4 d5 3.e5 c5 4.c3 Nc6 5.Nf3 Qb6`.\n\n**Idea:** black lets white have space but attacks the white pawn chain at its base with `...c5` and `...f6`. The bishop on c8 is often black's problem piece, so plan how to activate it.\n\n#### Caro-Kann Defence: very solid\n\n```\n1.e4 c6\n2.d4 d5\n3.Nc3 dxe4\n4.Nxe4 Bf5\n5.Ng3 Bg6\n6.h4 h6\n7.Nf3 Nd7\n```\n\n**Idea:** like the French, but the c8 bishop gets out before `...e6`. Black has a healthy pawn structure and good endgames. A great choice if you like safe positions.\n\n#### Scandinavian Defence: simple to learn\n\n```\n1.e4 d5\n2.exd5 Qxd5\n3.Nc3 Qa5\n4.d4 Nf6\n5.Nf3 Bf5\n```\n\n**Idea:** black takes the centre pawn at once. The queen comes out early, but from a5 it is safe. Easy to play because white's choices are limited.\n\n---\n\n### Openings with 1.d4\n\n#### Queen's Gambit Declined: classical and solid\n\n```\n1.d4 d5\n2.c4 e6\n3.Nc3 Nf6\n4.Bg5 Be7\n5.e3 O-O\n6.Nf3 h6\n```\n\n**Idea:** white offers the c4 pawn to pull black's d5 pawn away from the centre. Black declines and keeps a strong d5. Watch out for the **Elephant trap** in [chapter 1](#chapter-1-fastest-checkmates-and-opening-traps).\n\n#### Queen's Gambit Accepted\n\n```\n1.d4 d5\n2.c4 dxc4\n3.Nf3 Nf6\n4.e3 e6\n5.Bxc4 c5\n6.O-O a6\n```\n\n**Idea:** black takes the pawn, but does **not** try to keep it. White wins it back and black uses the time to develop. Trying to keep the pawn with `...b5` usually goes wrong, for example `1.d4 d5 2.c4 dxc4 3.e3 b5? 4.a4 c6 5.axb5 cxb5 6.Qf3`, attacking the rook on a8.\n\n#### Slav Defence\n\n```\n1.d4 d5\n2.c4 c6\n3.Nf3 Nf6\n4.Nc3 dxc4\n5.a4 Bf5\n```\n\n**Idea:** black supports d5 with the c-pawn, keeping the c8 bishop free to come out. Solid, and the structure is similar to the Caro-Kann, so the two fit well together.\n\n#### London System: one setup against almost everything\n\n```\n1.d4 d5\n2.Bf4 Nf6\n3.e3 e6\n4.Nf3 c5\n5.c3 Nc6\n6.Nbd2 Bd6\n7.Bg3 O-O\n8.Bd3\n```\n\n**Idea:** white plays the same moves (`d4`, `Bf4`, `e3`, `Nf3`, `c3`, `Nbd2`, `Bd3`) whatever black does. Very little theory, a safe king and a slow attack later with `Ne5`, `Qf3` or `h4`. A good choice if you don't have time to study openings.\n\n#### King's Indian Defence: let white have the centre, then attack it\n\n```\n1.d4 Nf6\n2.c4 g6\n3.Nc3 Bg7\n4.e4 d6\n5.Nf3 O-O\n6.Be2 e5\n7.O-O Nc6\n8.d5 Ne7\n```\n\n**Idea:** black castles quickly, then strikes with `...e5` and often `...f5`, attacking white's king on the kingside while white attacks on the queenside. Sharp and exciting.\n\n#### Nimzo-Indian Defence\n\n```\n1.d4 Nf6\n2.c4 e6\n3.Nc3 Bb4\n4.e3 O-O\n5.Bd3 d5\n6.Nf3 c5\n```\n\n**Idea:** the bishop pins the c3 knight so it cannot support `e4`. Black is often happy to give the bishop for the knight to damage white's pawns.\n\n---\n\n### Other first moves\n\n#### English Opening\n\n```\n1.c4 e5\n2.Nc3 Nc6\n3.g3 g6\n4.Bg2 Bg7\n5.Rb1 d6\n```\n\n**Idea:** white controls d5 from the side and the g2 bishop presses on the long diagonal. A flexible, positional opening.\n\n---\n\n### How to learn an opening (the professional way)\n\n1. **Learn the first 5 to 8 moves, not 20.**\n2. **For each move, ask why it is played.** What square does it control? Which piece does it free?\n3. **Know the typical plan** (pawn breaks like `d4`, `...c5`, `...f5`) and where the pieces belong.\n4. **Play it 10 to 20 times** against the robot. After each game, look at the 📜 move list and find the first move where you left your plan.\n5. **Learn the traps** in your opening, for both sides.\n6. **Add a new line only when an opponent surprises you.**\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 3: Thinking like a professional\n\nThe biggest difference between a beginner and a master is not memory. It is **how they think before each move**. Masters follow a routine so they rarely miss something simple.\n\n---\n\n### The thinking routine for every move\n\n#### Step 1: What did my opponent's last move do?\n\nBefore you look at your own ideas, ask:\n\n- Does it **attack** something of mine?\n- Does it **threaten** a check, a capture or a mate next move?\n- What square or line did it **open** or **leave unguarded**?\n\nMost beginner losses come from skipping this step.\n\n#### Step 2: Checks, captures, threats (CCT)\n\nLook at every **check**, every **capture** and every **threat** for both sides. Forcing moves are where tactics hide. Masters look at them first, even ones that look silly.\n\n#### Step 3: Choose 2 or 3 candidate moves\n\nDon't play the first good move you see. Find two or three reasonable moves, then compare them. A well-known saying: *\"When you see a good move, look for a better one.\"*\n\n#### Step 4: Calculate the reply\n\nFor each candidate, ask: **what is the opponent's best answer?** Assume they will find it. Look a couple of moves deep along forcing lines.\n\n#### Step 5: Blunder check\n\nBefore you let go of the piece, ask one last time:\n\n- After my move, is any of my pieces **undefended** and attackable?\n- Is my **back rank** safe?\n- Can they give a **check** that wins something?\n\n> 🧠 In My Ihsan you drop a piece by tapping the target square. Make the blunder check a habit before that final tap.\n\n---\n\n### Evaluating a position like a master\n\nWhen there is no tactic, masters judge the position using these factors:\n\n| Factor | Questions to ask |\n|---|---|\n| **Material** | Who has more? Pawn = 1, knight = 3, bishop = 3, rook = 5, queen = 9. |\n| **King safety** | Is either king exposed, uncastled or short of defenders? |\n| **Piece activity** | Which pieces are doing nothing? Which side has more active pieces? |\n| **Pawn structure** | Isolated, doubled or backward pawns? Passed pawns? |\n| **Space** | Who controls more squares in the centre and the opponent's half? |\n| **Weak squares** | Holes that no pawn can defend, perfect outposts for a knight. |\n\nThen make a **plan**: find your worst piece and improve it, or attack where you are stronger.\n\n---\n\n### Professional habits\n\n1. **Think in plans, not single moves.** \"I will put my knight on d5, then push f4\" is better than looking for random threats.\n2. **Prophylaxis.** Ask \"what does my opponent want to do?\" and stop it before it happens. This was the trademark of champions like Petrosian and Karpov.\n3. **Trade when you are ahead, avoid trades when you are behind.** With extra material, simplify into an easy endgame. When attacking, keep pieces on the board.\n4. **Every piece has a job.** If a piece has no job, find one for it.\n5. **Use your clock well.** Spend time at critical moments (when the position changes, after a capture, when you have many choices) and play quickly in simple positions.\n6. **Stay calm after a mistake.** Many games are won by the player who makes the second-to-last mistake. Keep fighting.\n7. **Respect your opponent.** Play every move as if they will find the best reply.\n\n---\n\n### How masters study\n\n| Method | How to do it |\n|---|---|\n| **Tactics puzzles every day** | 10 to 20 minutes daily. Pattern recognition is the fastest way to improve. |\n| **Endgames** | Learn the basic mates and king-and-pawn endings early (see chapter 4). |\n| **Review your own games** | After each game, find the move where you went wrong, without an engine first. |\n| **Play through master games** | Guess each move before you look at the answer. |\n| **Play longer games** | Slow games (15+10 or 30+0) teach more than fast ones. |\n\n#### A famous game to study: the Opera Game\n\nPaul Morphy beat two noble amateurs at the Paris opera in 1858. It shows rapid development, open lines and a beautiful finish. Play it through on a real board or in the 2-player mode.\n\n```\n1.e4 e5        2.Nf3 d6       3.d4 Bg4\n4.dxe5 Bxf3    5.Qxf3 dxe5    6.Bc4 Nf6\n7.Qb3 Qe7      8.Nc3 c6       9.Bg5 b5\n10.Nxb5 cxb5   11.Bxb5+ Nbd7  12.O-O-O Rd8\n13.Rxd7 Rxd7   14.Rd1 Qe6     15.Bxd7+ Nxd7\n16.Qb8+ Nxb8   17.Rd8#\n```\n\n**What to notice:**\n\n- Black's `3...Bg4` and `4...Bxf3` give up a bishop for a knight and lose time.\n- By move 12 every white piece is developed and the king is castled. Black's pieces on f8 and h8 never moved.\n- White gives up material (the knight, then the bishop, then the queen) to keep the attack. Development is worth more than material when the opponent's king is in the centre.\n- The final mate uses only a rook and a bishop.\n\n---\n\n### Common beginner mistakes and the pro fix\n\n| Mistake | Pro fix |\n|---|---|\n| Moving the same piece many times in the opening | Develop a new piece each move |\n| Bringing the queen out early | Develop knights and bishops first |\n| Not castling | Castle within the first 10 moves |\n| Grabbing every pawn | Ask why it is free; development may be worth more |\n| Only looking at your own plan | Always check the opponent's last move first |\n| Attacking with one piece | Bring more pieces before you attack |\n| Giving up when behind | Look for tricks, stalemate ideas and your opponent's mistakes |\n| Playing too fast | Use the blunder check before every move |\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 4: Strategy and tactics\n\n> *\"Tactics is knowing what to do when there is something to do. Strategy is knowing what to do when there is nothing to do.\"* (attributed to Savielly Tartakower)\n\nA game has three phases: the **opening** (about the first 10 to 15 moves), the **middlegame** and the **endgame**. Each one needs a different way of thinking.\n\n---\n\n### Part A: Opening principles\n\n1. **Control the centre** (d4, d5, e4, e5) with pawns and pieces.\n2. **Develop knights and bishops** quickly, knights usually before bishops.\n3. **Castle early** to keep the king safe and bring a rook to the centre.\n4. **Don't move a piece twice** without a good reason.\n5. **Don't bring the queen out too early.** It gets chased and you lose time.\n6. **Connect your rooks** (nothing standing between them on the back rank).\n7. **Don't grab pawns** at the cost of development.\n\n---\n\n### Part B: Tactics, the patterns that win material\n\nMost games between beginners and club players are decided by tactics. Learn to spot these patterns.\n\n| Pattern | What it is | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Fork** | One piece attacks two or more pieces at once | A knight checks the king and attacks the queen |\n| **Pin** | A piece cannot move without exposing a more valuable piece behind it | A bishop pins a knight to the king |\n| **Skewer** | Like a pin, but the more valuable piece is in front and must move | A rook checks the king, and the queen behind it falls |\n| **Discovered attack** | Moving one piece opens a line for another piece to attack | Moving a knight reveals a bishop's check |\n| **Double check** | Two pieces give check at once; the king must move | Often leads to mate |\n| **Removing the defender** | Capture or chase away the piece that guards something | Take the knight that defends the back rank |\n| **Deflection** | Force a defender away from its duty | A sacrifice that drags the queen off a key square |\n| **Decoy** | Lure a piece onto a bad square | Sacrifice to pull the king into a check |\n| **Overloading** | One piece is defending two things; attack both | Then it can only save one |\n| **Zwischenzug** | An \"in-between\" move, often a check, before the expected recapture | Check first, then take back |\n\n**Training tip:** knights are the best forking pieces because they jump. Every time a knight can reach a square that attacks two valuable pieces, check it.\n\n---\n\n### Part C: Mating patterns\n\nRecognise these finishing shapes and you will find mates much faster.\n\n#### Back-rank mate\n\nThe king is trapped behind its own pawns (for example on g8 with pawns on f7, g7, h7), and a rook or queen mates on the back rank.\n\n**Defence:** make a \"luft\" (an escape square) by moving a pawn such as `h6` / `h3`, or keep a rook on the back rank.\n\n#### Smothered mate\n\nA knight gives check and the king cannot move because its own pieces surround it. Seen in the Blackburne, Caro-Kann and Kieninger traps in [chapter 1](#chapter-1-fastest-checkmates-and-opening-traps).\n\n#### Arabian mate\n\nA rook and knight mate a king in the corner: the rook gives check next to the king (for example on h7 against a king on h8), protected by the knight, which also covers g8. One of the oldest known patterns, already described in Arabic manuscripts of shatranj.\n\n#### Anastasia's mate\n\nA knight on e7 covers g8 and g6, and a rook or queen mates on the h-file against a king on h7, whose own pawn on g7 blocks its escape.\n\n#### Boden's mate\n\nTwo bishops on crossing diagonals mate a king that has castled queenside, often after a queen sacrifice on c6 or c3.\n\n#### Ladder (lawnmower) mate\n\nTwo rooks (or a rook and queen) take turns pushing the king to the edge, one rank at a time, then mate it there. The easiest way to finish when you are far ahead.\n\n#### Queen and king mate\n\nThe queen gives mate on a square next to the enemy king, protected by its own king or another piece.\n\n---\n\n### Part D: Middlegame strategy\n\nWhen there is no tactic, follow a plan.\n\n#### 1. Find your worst piece and improve it\n\nA bishop blocked by its own pawns, a knight on the edge (\"a knight on the rim is dim\"), a rook on a closed file: move these to better squares.\n\n#### 2. Rooks belong on open files\n\nAn **open file** has no pawns. A **half-open file** has only the opponent's pawn. Put rooks there, and double them if you can. A rook on the 7th rank (2nd for black) is very strong.\n\n#### 3. Knights love outposts\n\nAn **outpost** is a square in the opponent's half that no enemy pawn can attack, ideally supported by your own pawn. A knight on d5 or e5 that cannot be chased is often worth more than a bishop.\n\n#### 4. Bishops love open positions\n\nTwo bishops (the \"bishop pair\") are strong in open positions with few pawns. In closed positions knights are often better.\n\n#### 5. Pawn structure\n\n| Weakness | What it means | How to use it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Isolated pawn** | No friendly pawn on the files beside it | Block it with a piece, then attack it |\n| **Doubled pawns** | Two pawns on the same file | They defend each other badly |\n| **Backward pawn** | Cannot be protected by a neighbour and cannot advance safely | Put pressure on it and the square in front |\n| **Passed pawn** | No enemy pawn can stop it on its way to promotion | \"Passed pawns must be pushed.\" Support it with pieces |\n\n#### 6. Attack where you are stronger\n\nPawns point the way. If your pawn chain points to the kingside (for example white pawns on d4 and e5), attack on the kingside. Attack the king only when you have more pieces near it than the defender.\n\n#### 7. Pawn breaks\n\nA **pawn break** is a pawn move that challenges the opponent's chain and opens lines (for example `...c5` or `...f6` in the French, `...f5` in the King's Indian). Most plans are built around one key break.\n\n#### 8. Space\n\nIf you have more space, avoid trades so your opponent stays cramped. If you are cramped, trade pieces to get room.\n\n---\n\n### Part E: Endgames\n\nIn the endgame the **king becomes a fighting piece**. Bring it to the centre.\n\n#### Must-know mates\n\n**King and queen vs king.** Use the queen to shrink the enemy king's box a knight's move away from it, bring your own king close, then mate on the edge. Careful: always leave the enemy king at least one move, or it is **stalemate** (a draw).\n\n**King and rook vs king.** Use the rook to cut the king off rank by rank. Bring your king opposite the enemy king (with one square between), then check with the rook along the edge.\n\n**Two rooks vs king.** The ladder mate from Part C.\n\n#### King and pawn endings\n\n- **Opposition:** when the kings face each other with one square between them, the side that does *not* have to move usually wins the fight. This decides many pawn endings.\n- **Rule of the square:** draw a square from the pawn to its promotion rank. If the defending king can step inside that square, it catches the pawn.\n- **King in front of the pawn:** when promoting a pawn with your king's help, put the king in front of the pawn, not behind it.\n\n#### Rook endings\n\nThe most common endings. Two famous ideas:\n\n- **Lucena position (winning):** build a \"bridge\" with your rook to shield your king from checks and promote the pawn.\n- **Philidor position (drawing):** the defender keeps the rook on their third rank until the pawn advances, then checks from behind.\n- And a rule: **rooks belong behind passed pawns**, both your own and the opponent's.\n\n#### Draw rules the game knows\n\nMy Ihsan's engine plays the full rules, so remember these when you are behind:\n\n- **Stalemate** (buntu): no legal move and not in check. Draw.\n- **Threefold repetition**: the same position three times. Draw.\n- **50-move rule**: 50 moves each with no capture and no pawn move. Draw.\n- **Insufficient material**: for example king and bishop vs king. Draw.\n\nWhen losing, look for a stalemate trick or a repetition. When winning, avoid them.\n\n---\n\n### Strategy cheat sheet\n\n| Phase | Main goal | Key question |\n|---|---|---|\n| Opening | Develop, centre, castle | \"Have I developed all my pieces?\" |\n| Middlegame | Tactics and plans | \"What is my worst piece? Where should I attack?\" |\n| Endgame | Active king, passed pawns | \"Can I make a passed pawn? Is my king active?\" |\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 5: Training plan in My Ihsan\n\nHow to use the features of **Explore › 3D chess** to improve step by step.\n\n### The tools you have\n\n| Feature | How it helps you train |\n|---|---|\n| 🤖 **Robot level 1 · Easy** | Practise opening principles and the quick traps from chapter 1 |\n| 🤖 **Robot level 2 · Medium** | Practise your openings and basic tactics |\n| 🤖 **Robot level 3 · Hard** | Real games: play slowly and use the thinking routine |\n| ⚪ / ⚫ / 🔀 **I play** | Practise both colours; your black openings need training too |\n| ⏱ **Clock** (None, 3+2, 5+0, 10+0, 15+10, 30+0) | Start with no clock or 30+0; use 3+2 and 5+0 only for fun |\n| ↶ **Undo** | Take back a blunder and find the right move (training games only) |\n| 🔄 **Flip board** | See the position from your opponent's side to spot their threats |\n| 📜 **Move list** | Review the game and find where it turned |\n| 👥 **2 players** | Play friends and family, or set up famous games and play through them |\n| ↩ **Resume** | The game is saved, so you can think about a hard position later |\n| 🏆 **Won / Lost / Drawn** | Your record against the robot is shown on the chess banner |\n| 📖 **Guide** | Every line in this guide can be played on a board, then carried on in 3D |\n\n---\n\n### Week 1: Basics and quick traps\n\n- **Learn notation** from the [guide index](#how-to-read-the-moves). Watch the 📜 move list as you play.\n- Play **Fool's mate and Scholar's mate** in 2-player mode to see how they work, then play the defence to each.\n- Play **5 games against robot level 1** as white, using the Italian Game. Goal: castle in every game.\n- Learn the **king and queen vs king** mate. Practise until you can do it without stalemate.\n\n### Week 2: Openings and tactics\n\n- Pick **one opening as white** and **one reply to 1.e4** from [chapter 2](#chapter-2-popular-openings).\n- Play **5 games against level 2** with each colour.\n- Every day, spend 10 minutes on the tactical patterns in [chapter 4](#chapter-4-strategy-and-tactics): forks, pins and skewers.\n- After each game, open the move list and write down the first move where you lost material.\n\n### Week 3: Thinking routine\n\n- Play **level 2 with a 30+0 clock**. Before every move, follow the routine in [chapter 3](#chapter-3-thinking-like-a-professional): opponent's last move → checks, captures, threats → candidates → blunder check.\n- Use **Undo** only after the game ends: go back to your mistake and find a better move.\n- Learn the **king and rook vs king** mate and the **rule of the square**.\n- Play through the **Opera Game** in 2-player mode, guessing white's moves.\n\n### Week 4: Real games\n\n- Play **level 3 with 15+10**. No undo during the game.\n- Add **one reply to 1.d4** to your repertoire.\n- Review every loss: was it a tactic you missed (chapter 4, Part B), a bad plan (Part D) or the clock?\n- Goal: your first win against level 3.\n\n---\n\n### Daily 20-minute routine\n\n| Minutes | Activity |\n|---|---|\n| 5 | Review one trap or mating pattern from chapters 1 and 4 |\n| 10 | One game against the robot (or a few moves of a longer saved game) |\n| 5 | Look at the move list and find your best and worst move |\n\n---\n\n### Checklist before every move ✅\n\n1. What did my opponent just do?\n2. Any checks, captures or threats, for me or for them?\n3. Do I have 2 or 3 candidate moves?\n4. What is their best reply?\n5. Is anything of mine left undefended? Is my back rank safe?\n\nGood luck, and enjoy the game. ♟️\n", "ms": "# ♟️ Panduan Catur My Ihsan\n\nPanduan langkah demi langkah untuk pemain **Teroka › Catur 3D** dalam My Ihsan. Baca bab mengikut susunan, atau terus ke bab yang anda perlukan.\n\n| # | Bab | Apa yang anda pelajari |\n|---|-----|------------------------|\n| 1 | [Mat terpantas dan perangkap pembukaan](#chapter-1) | Fool's mate, Scholar's mate, mat Légal dan kemenangan cepat lain, serta cara menghalang setiap satu |\n| 2 | [Pembukaan popular](#chapter-2) | Pembukaan yang paling banyak digunakan pemain kuat, langkah pertamanya dan idea di sebaliknya |\n| 3 | [Berfikir seperti pemain profesional](#chapter-3) | Cara master memilih langkah, membaca notasi, menyemak kesilapan dan mengkaji permainan |\n| 4 | [Strategi dan taktik](#chapter-4) | Prinsip pembukaan, rancangan pertengahan, corak taktik, corak mat dan penamat |\n| 5 | [Pelan latihan dalam My Ihsan](#chapter-5) | Pelan 4 minggu menggunakan tahap robot, jam, undur dan senarai langkah |\n\nSetiap baris langkah dalam panduan ini telah dimainkan melalui enjin catur My Ihsan untuk memastikan ia sah dan setiap mat benar-benar mat. Tekan **▶ Main di papan** di bawah mana-mana baris untuk melihatnya langkah demi langkah.\n\n## Cara membaca langkah {#chapter-0}\n\nSenarai langkah dalam permainan (📜 Langkah) menggunakan **notasi algebra standard**, begitu juga panduan ini.\n\n- Petak dinamakan mengikut **fail** (a–h, kiri ke kanan dari sisi putih) dan **baris** (1–8, dari sisi putih). Raja putih bermula di **e1**, raja hitam di **e8**.\n- Buah (huruf Inggeris, standard antarabangsa): **K** raja, **Q** menteri, **R** tir, **B** gajah, **N** kuda. Bidak tiada huruf: `e4` bermaksud bidak bergerak ke e4.\n- `x` = tangkap (`Bxf7` = gajah tangkap di f7), `+` = sah, `#` = mat.\n- `O-O` = istana sebelah raja, `O-O-O` = istana sebelah menteri.\n- `1.e4 e5` = langkah pertama putih, kemudian balasan hitam. `3...Nf6` = langkah ketiga hitam.\n- `?` = kesilapan, `??` = kesilapan besar, `!` = langkah bagus.\n\n## Nama buah dalam permainan\n\n| Simbol | Inggeris | Tema klasik (BM) | Tema istana Melayu |\n|:-:|---|---|---|\n| ♚ K | King | Raja | Sultan |\n| ♛ Q | Queen | Menteri | Permaisuri |\n| ♜ R | Rook | Tir | Laksamana |\n| ♝ B | Bishop | Gajah | Bendahara |\n| ♞ N | Knight | Kuda | Hulubalang |\n| ♟ P | Pawn | Bidak | Pesilat |\n\n| Inggeris | Bahasa Melayu |\n|---|---|\n| Check | Sah |\n| Checkmate | Mat |\n| Stalemate | Buntu |\n| Draw | Seri |\n| Castling | Istana |\n| Promotion | Naik pangkat |\n\n## Sedikit sejarah\n\nCatur sampai ke dunia Islam sebagai *shatranj* dari Parsi, dan selama beberapa abad pemain dan penulis catur terkuat tinggal di wilayah Abbasiyah. **Al-Adli ar-Rumi** (abad ke-9) menulis antara buku catur terawal yang diketahui, dan **as-Suli** (wafat sekitar 946) ialah juara zamannya di Baghdad; kajian penamatnya masih dibincangkan hingga hari ini. \"Mat Arab\" dalam bab 4 dinamakan sempena tradisi ini.\n\n---\n\n## Bab 1: Mat terpantas dan perangkap pembukaan {#chapter-1}\n\nInilah cara paling cepat permainan boleh tamat. Pelajarinya atas dua sebab:\n\n1. **Supaya ia tidak berlaku kepada anda.** Kebanyakannya hanya berjaya jika pihak lawan membuat langkah buruk.\n2. **Untuk menghukum pemula.** Menentang robot tahap 1, atau kawan yang baru belajar, ia boleh menang dalam beberapa langkah.\n\n> ⚠️ Lawan yang baik tidak akan terpedaya. Jika anda hanya bermain untuk perangkap cepat, kedudukan anda sering menjadi lebih teruk. Gunakannya sebagai bonus, dan mainkan pembukaan yang kukuh dari [bab 2](#chapter-2) sebagai rancangan utama.\n\nTitik lemah dalam setiap perangkap ini sama: petak di sebelah raja yang hanya dilindungi oleh raja, terutamanya **f7** (untuk hitam) dan **f2** (untuk putih) pada awal permainan.\n\n---\n\n### Fool's mate: 2 langkah (paling cepat)\n\n```\n1.f3 e5\n2.g4 Qh4#\n```\n\nPutih membuka pepenjuru dari h4 ke e1 dengan menggerakkan bidak f dan g, dan menteri hitam terus mat. Perkara sama berlaku selepas `1.f4 e5 2.g4 Qh4#`.\n\nPutih juga boleh menggunakan idea yang sama: `1.e4 g5 2.d4 f6 3.Qh5#`.\n\n**Pengajaran:** pada awal permainan, jangan gerakkan bidak f dan g di depan raja tanpa sebab.\n\n---\n\n### Scholar's mate: 4 langkah\n\n```\n1.e4 e5\n2.Bc4 Nc6\n3.Qh5 Nf6??\n4.Qxf7#\n```\n\nMenteri dan gajah putih sama-sama menghala ke f7. Hitam hanya melindungi e5 dan terlupa f7.\n\nVersi dengan menteri di f3: `1.e4 e5 2.Qf3 Nc6 3.Bc4 d6?? 4.Qxf7#`.\n\n#### Cara menghalang Scholar's mate\n\nSelepas `3.Qh5`, hitam patut melindungi e5 **dan** menghalang menteri:\n\n```\n1.e4 e5\n2.Bc4 Nc6\n3.Qh5 g6!\n4.Qf3 Nf6!\n5.Qb3 Qe7\n6.Nc3 Nd4\n```\n\nSekarang menteri putih telah dihalau ke sana sini, hitam berkembang sambil membuat ancaman setiap langkah, dan hitam lebih baik. **Mengeluarkan menteri terlalu awal ialah kelemahan apabila lawan tahu cara bertahan.**\n\nPeraturan paling mudah: selepas gajah putih ke c4, mainkan `...Nf6` awal supaya kuda mengawal h5 dan menteri tidak boleh ke situ.\n\n---\n\n### Mat Légal: 7 langkah\n\nDinamakan sempena master Perancis abad ke-18, Légal. Ia menunjukkan idea terkenal: **mengorbankan menteri untuk mat dengan buah kecil.**\n\n```\n1.e4 e5\n2.Nf3 d6\n3.Bc4 Bg4\n4.Nc3 g6?\n5.Nxe5! Bxd1??\n6.Bxf7+ Ke7\n7.Nd5#\n```\n\nHitam sangka kuda di f3 terpaku kepada menteri lalu menangkap menteri, tetapi gajah dan dua kuda putih mat raja. Hitam patut main `5...dxe5`, yang hanya kehilangan satu bidak (selepas `6.Qxg4`).\n\n**Pengajaran:** pakuan kepada menteri bukan pakuan sebenar. Buah itu *boleh* bergerak jika ganjarannya lebih besar daripada menteri.\n\n---\n\n### Perangkap Blackburne Shilling: 7 langkah (hitam menang)\n\nDinamakan sempena master Inggeris Joseph Blackburne, yang dikatakan memenangi taruhan kecil dengannya. (Dalam My Ihsan kita belajar polanya sahaja, tanpa sebarang taruhan.)\n\n```\n1.e4 e5\n2.Nf3 Nc6\n3.Bc4 Nd4\n4.Nxe5? Qg5!\n5.Nxf7?? Qxg2\n6.Rf1 Qxe4+\n7.Be2 Nf3#\n```\n\n`3...Nd4` bukan langkah yang baik, tetapi ia memasang perangkap. Putih merampas bidak, kemudian tir di f7, dan terkena **mat lemas** (smothered mate): raja terkurung oleh buahnya sendiri.\n\n**Cara putih mengelak:** balas `3...Nd4` dengan `4.Nxd4 exd4 5.c3` atau sekadar `4.O-O`. Jangan ambil bidak e5.\n\n---\n\n### Perangkap Englund Gambit: 8 langkah (hitam menang)\n\n```\n1.d4 e5\n2.dxe5 Nc6\n3.Nf3 Qe7\n4.Bf4? Qb4+\n5.Bd2 Qxb2\n6.Bc3? Bb4\n7.Qd2 Bxc3\n8.Qxc3 Qc1#\n```\n\nMenteri hitam merampas bidak b2 dan barisan belakang putih runtuh. Putih patut main `4.Qd5` atau `4.Nc3` dan bukannya `4.Bf4`.\n\n---\n\n### Mat lemas dalam Caro-Kann: 6 langkah\n\n```\n1.e4 c6\n2.d4 d5\n3.Nc3 dxe4\n4.Nxe4 Nd7\n5.Qe2 Ngf6??\n6.Nd6#\n```\n\nMenteri di e2 memaku bidak e7 kepada raja, jadi `Nd6` tidak boleh ditangkap. Raja hitam dikelilingi buahnya sendiri. Hitam patut main `5...Ndf6`, supaya kuda d7 beredar dan raja mendapat petak.\n\n**Pengajaran:** apabila menteri lawan berada di garisan yang sama dengan raja anda, berhati-hati supaya tidak mengurung raja sendiri.\n\n---\n\n### Perangkap Kieninger (Budapest Gambit): 8 langkah (hitam menang)\n\n```\n1.d4 Nf6\n2.c4 e5\n3.dxe5 Ng4\n4.Bf4 Nc6\n5.Nf3 Bb4+\n6.Nbd2 Qe7\n7.a3 Ngxe5\n8.axb4?? Nd3#\n```\n\nMenangkap gajah membuka laluan untuk mat lemas di d3: raja putih terhalang oleh menteri dan kudanya sendiri. Putih patut main `8.Nxe5 Nxe5 9.e3` dan kekal selamat.\n\n---\n\n### Serangan Fried Liver (bukan mat, tetapi serangan kuat)\n\nKegemaran pemain kelab. Ia bermula begini:\n\n```\n1.e4 e5\n2.Nf3 Nc6\n3.Bc4 Nf6\n4.Ng5 d5\n5.exd5 Nxd5?\n6.Nxf7! Kxf7\n7.Qf3+ Ke6\n8.Nc3\n```\n\nRaja hitam ditarik ke tengah papan dan putih menyerang. **Pertahanan hitam yang betul** ialah tidak menangkap semula dengan kuda:\n\n```\n5...Na5!\n6.Bb5+ c6\n7.dxc6 bxc6\n8.Be2 h6\n```\n\nHitam memberi satu bidak, tetapi menghalau buah putih dan berkembang dengan cepat.\n\n---\n\n### Perangkap yang memenangi menteri\n\n#### Perangkap Petrov\n\n```\n1.e4 e5\n2.Nf3 Nf6\n3.Nxe5 Nxe4?\n4.Qe2 Nf6??\n5.Nc6+\n```\n\nKuda bergerak dengan **sah tersingkap** (discovered check) daripada menteri di e2 dan pada masa sama menyerang menteri hitam di d8. Hitam kehilangan menteri. (Selepas `3.Nxe5` hitam patut main `3...d6` dahulu, kemudian tangkap di e4.)\n\n#### Perangkap Gajah (Elephant trap, Queen's Gambit Declined)\n\n```\n1.d4 d5\n2.c4 e6\n3.Nc3 Nf6\n4.Bg5 Nbd7\n5.cxd5 exd5\n6.Nxd5? Nxd5!\n7.Bxd8 Bb4+\n8.Qd2 Bxd2+\n9.Kxd2 Kxd8\n```\n\nPutih sangka kuda di f6 terpaku lalu merampas bidak. Hitam memberi menteri, merampasnya kembali dengan sah, dan akhirnya **lebih satu buah**.\n\n---\n\n### Ringkasan: cara tidak kalah kepada perangkap cepat\n\n1. **Lindungi f7 / f2** pada langkah awal. Awasi menteri atau gajah yang menghala ke situ.\n2. **Jangan gerakkan bidak f dan g** di depan raja terlalu awal.\n3. **Kembangkan kuda dan gajah sebelum menteri.**\n4. **Beristana awal** untuk mengalihkan raja dari tengah.\n5. **Sebelum merampas bidak atau buah \"percuma\", tanya: kenapa ia percuma?**\n6. **Pakuan kepada menteri boleh dipecahkan** jika ganjarannya mat atau lebih banyak bahan.\n\n---\n\n## Bab 2: Pembukaan popular {#chapter-2}\n\nAnda tidak perlu menghafal baris yang panjang. Pemain kuat mengingat **idea** sesuatu pembukaan dan beberapa langkah pertama. Jika lawan keluar dari buku, anda masih tahu apa yang hendak dibuat.\n\n**Mula dengan satu pembukaan untuk putih dan dua balasan untuk hitam** (satu menentang `1.e4`, satu menentang `1.d4`). Mainkannya berulang kali menentang robot.\n\n| Jika anda suka... | Main dengan putih | Hitam menentang 1.e4 | Hitam menentang 1.d4 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Permainan klasik yang mudah | Italian Game | 1...e5 (Italian / Ruy Lopez) | Queen's Gambit Declined |\n| Sistem dengan sedikit baris untuk dihafal | London System | Caro-Kann | Slav Defence |\n| Permainan tajam dan menyerang | Scotch Game | Sicilian Defence | King's Indian Defence |\n\n---\n\n### Pembukaan dengan 1.e4\n\n#### Italian Game (Giuoco Piano): pembukaan pertama terbaik\n\n```\n1.e4 e5\n2.Nf3 Nc6\n3.Bc4 Bc5\n4.c3 Nf6\n5.d3 d6\n6.O-O O-O\n```\n\n**Idea untuk putih:** gajah di c4 menghala ke f7, `c3` menyediakan `d4` untuk menguasai tengah, dan `d3` yang perlahan memastikan semuanya selamat. Kemudian putih sering main `Re1`, `Nbd2-f1-g3` dan menolak `d4` atau `b4`.\n\n**Idea untuk hitam:** tiru susunan sihat yang sama, beristana, dan cari `...d5` atau `...a6` dengan `...Ba7`.\n\nBaris berkaitan ialah **Two Knights Defence** (`3...Nf6`). Menentang `4.d3 Be7 5.O-O O-O` permainannya tenang. Jika putih main `4.Ng5`, lihat Fried Liver dalam [bab 1](#chapter-1).\n\n#### Ruy Lopez (Pembukaan Sepanyol): pembukaan juara dunia\n\n```\n1.e4 e5\n2.Nf3 Nc6\n3.Bb5 a6\n4.Ba4 Nf6\n5.O-O Be7\n6.Re1 b5\n7.Bb3 d6\n8.c3 O-O\n```\n\n**Idea:** gajah di b5 menyerang kuda yang mempertahankan e5. Putih membina perlahan-lahan dengan `c3` dan `d4`, dan permainan menjadi perebutan panjang di tengah. Ia kaya dan mendalam, jadi sesuai sebagai pembukaan kedua selepas anda selesa dengan Italian.\n\n#### Scotch Game: buka tengah serta-merta\n\n```\n1.e4 e5\n2.Nf3 Nc6\n3.d4 exd4\n4.Nxd4 Nf6\n5.Nxc6 bxc6\n6.e5 Qe7\n```\n\n**Idea:** putih membuka garisan dengan segera dan buahnya aktif. Sesuai untuk pemain yang suka taktik.\n\n#### Sicilian Defence: balasan hitam paling popular untuk bertarung\n\n**Najdorf** (kegemaran Fischer dan Kasparov):\n\n```\n1.e4 c5\n2.Nf3 d6\n3.d4 cxd4\n4.Nxd4 Nf6\n5.Nc3 a6\n```\n\n**Dragon** (gajah g7 seperti hembusan naga di sepanjang pepenjuru):\n\n```\n1.e4 c5\n2.Nf3 d6\n3.d4 cxd4\n4.Nxd4 Nf6\n5.Nc3 g6\n6.Be3 Bg7\n7.f3 O-O\n8.Qd2 Nc6\n```\n\n**Idea:** hitam menukar bidak c dengan bidak d putih, jadi kedudukan tidak seimbang. Hitam sering menyerang di sebelah menteri melalui fail c yang separa terbuka, manakala putih menyerang raja. Permainannya tajam, jadi fahami ideanya sebelum memainkannya.\n\nJika anda putih dan tidak mahu mempelajari baris Sicilian yang besar, cuba **Alapin**: `1.e4 c5 2.c3 Nf6 3.e5 Nd5 4.d4 cxd4 5.Nf3 Nc6`.\n\n#### French Defence: kukuh dan menyerang balas\n\n```\n1.e4 e6\n2.d4 d5\n3.Nc3 Nf6\n4.Bg5 Be7\n5.e5 Nfd7\n```\n\n**Variasi Advance**: `1.e4 e6 2.d4 d5 3.e5 c5 4.c3 Nc6 5.Nf3 Qb6`.\n\n**Idea:** hitam membiarkan putih mendapat ruang tetapi menyerang rantai bidak putih di pangkalnya dengan `...c5` dan `...f6`. Gajah c8 sering menjadi buah bermasalah hitam, jadi rancang cara mengaktifkannya.\n\n#### Caro-Kann Defence: sangat kukuh\n\n```\n1.e4 c6\n2.d4 d5\n3.Nc3 dxe4\n4.Nxe4 Bf5\n5.Ng3 Bg6\n6.h4 h6\n7.Nf3 Nd7\n```\n\n**Idea:** seperti French, tetapi gajah c8 keluar sebelum `...e6`. Hitam mempunyai struktur bidak yang sihat dan penamat yang baik. Pilihan hebat jika anda suka kedudukan selamat.\n\n#### Scandinavian Defence: mudah dipelajari\n\n```\n1.e4 d5\n2.exd5 Qxd5\n3.Nc3 Qa5\n4.d4 Nf6\n5.Nf3 Bf5\n```\n\n**Idea:** hitam terus menangkap bidak tengah. Menteri keluar awal, tetapi di a5 ia selamat. Mudah dimainkan kerana pilihan putih terhad.\n\n---\n\n### Pembukaan dengan 1.d4\n\n#### Queen's Gambit Declined: klasik dan kukuh\n\n```\n1.d4 d5\n2.c4 e6\n3.Nc3 Nf6\n4.Bg5 Be7\n5.e3 O-O\n6.Nf3 h6\n```\n\n**Idea:** putih menawarkan bidak c4 untuk menarik bidak d5 hitam dari tengah. Hitam menolak dan mengekalkan d5 yang kuat. Awas **Perangkap Gajah** dalam [bab 1](#chapter-1).\n\n#### Queen's Gambit Accepted\n\n```\n1.d4 d5\n2.c4 dxc4\n3.Nf3 Nf6\n4.e3 e6\n5.Bxc4 c5\n6.O-O a6\n```\n\n**Idea:** hitam mengambil bidak, tetapi **tidak** cuba menyimpannya. Putih merampasnya kembali dan hitam menggunakan masa untuk berkembang. Cuba menyimpan bidak dengan `...b5` biasanya gagal, contohnya `1.d4 d5 2.c4 dxc4 3.e3 b5? 4.a4 c6 5.axb5 cxb5 6.Qf3`, menyerang tir di a8.\n\n#### Slav Defence\n\n```\n1.d4 d5\n2.c4 c6\n3.Nf3 Nf6\n4.Nc3 dxc4\n5.a4 Bf5\n```\n\n**Idea:** hitam menyokong d5 dengan bidak c, membiarkan gajah c8 bebas keluar. Kukuh, dan strukturnya serupa dengan Caro-Kann, jadi kedua-duanya sepadan.\n\n#### London System: satu susunan menentang hampir semua\n\n```\n1.d4 d5\n2.Bf4 Nf6\n3.e3 e6\n4.Nf3 c5\n5.c3 Nc6\n6.Nbd2 Bd6\n7.Bg3 O-O\n8.Bd3\n```\n\n**Idea:** putih main langkah yang sama (`d4`, `Bf4`, `e3`, `Nf3`, `c3`, `Nbd2`, `Bd3`) apa pun hitam buat. Teori sangat sedikit, raja selamat, dan serangan perlahan kemudian dengan `Ne5`, `Qf3` atau `h4`. Pilihan baik jika anda tiada masa untuk mengkaji pembukaan.\n\n#### King's Indian Defence: biarkan putih ambil tengah, kemudian serangnya\n\n```\n1.d4 Nf6\n2.c4 g6\n3.Nc3 Bg7\n4.e4 d6\n5.Nf3 O-O\n6.Be2 e5\n7.O-O Nc6\n8.d5 Ne7\n```\n\n**Idea:** hitam beristana dengan cepat, kemudian menyerang dengan `...e5` dan sering `...f5`, menyerang raja putih di sebelah raja sementara putih menyerang di sebelah menteri. Tajam dan menarik.\n\n#### Nimzo-Indian Defence\n\n```\n1.d4 Nf6\n2.c4 e6\n3.Nc3 Bb4\n4.e3 O-O\n5.Bd3 d5\n6.Nf3 c5\n```\n\n**Idea:** gajah memaku kuda c3 supaya ia tidak dapat menyokong `e4`. Hitam selalunya gembira menukar gajah dengan kuda untuk merosakkan bidak putih.\n\n---\n\n### Langkah pertama lain\n\n#### English Opening\n\n```\n1.c4 e5\n2.Nc3 Nc6\n3.g3 g6\n4.Bg2 Bg7\n5.Rb1 d6\n```\n\n**Idea:** putih mengawal d5 dari sisi dan gajah g2 menekan pepenjuru panjang. Pembukaan yang fleksibel dan berasaskan kedudukan.\n\n---\n\n### Cara mempelajari pembukaan (cara profesional)\n\n1. **Pelajari 5 hingga 8 langkah pertama, bukan 20.**\n2. **Untuk setiap langkah, tanya kenapa ia dimainkan.** Petak mana yang dikawal? Buah mana yang dibebaskan?\n3. **Ketahui rancangan biasa** (pecahan bidak seperti `d4`, `...c5`, `...f5`) dan tempat buah sepatutnya berada.\n4. **Mainkannya 10 hingga 20 kali** menentang robot. Selepas setiap permainan, lihat senarai 📜 langkah dan cari langkah pertama anda keluar dari rancangan.\n5. **Pelajari perangkap** dalam pembukaan anda, untuk kedua-dua pihak.\n6. **Tambah baris baharu hanya apabila lawan mengejutkan anda.**\n\n---\n\n## Bab 3: Berfikir seperti pemain profesional {#chapter-3}\n\nPerbezaan terbesar antara pemula dan master bukan ingatan. Ia ialah **cara mereka berfikir sebelum setiap langkah**. Master mengikut rutin supaya jarang terlepas perkara mudah.\n\n---\n\n### Rutin berfikir untuk setiap langkah\n\n#### Langkah 1: Apa yang dibuat oleh langkah terakhir lawan?\n\nSebelum melihat idea sendiri, tanya:\n\n- Adakah ia **menyerang** sesuatu milik saya?\n- Adakah ia **mengancam** sah, tangkapan atau mat pada langkah seterusnya?\n- Petak atau garisan apa yang ia **buka** atau **tinggalkan tanpa kawalan**?\n\nKebanyakan kekalahan pemula berpunca daripada melangkau langkah ini.\n\n#### Langkah 2: Sah, tangkapan, ancaman (CCT)\n\nLihat setiap **sah**, setiap **tangkapan** dan setiap **ancaman** untuk kedua-dua pihak. Langkah paksaan ialah tempat taktik bersembunyi. Master melihatnya dahulu, walaupun yang nampak pelik.\n\n#### Langkah 3: Pilih 2 atau 3 langkah calon\n\nJangan main langkah baik pertama yang anda nampak. Cari dua atau tiga langkah munasabah, kemudian bandingkan. Kata-kata terkenal: *\"Apabila anda nampak langkah yang baik, cari yang lebih baik.\"*\n\n#### Langkah 4: Kira balasan\n\nUntuk setiap calon, tanya: **apa balasan terbaik lawan?** Anggap mereka akan menjumpainya. Lihat beberapa langkah ke depan di sepanjang baris paksaan.\n\n#### Langkah 5: Semakan kesilapan\n\nSebelum melepaskan buah, tanya sekali lagi:\n\n- Selepas langkah saya, adakah mana-mana buah saya **tidak dipertahankan** dan boleh diserang?\n- Adakah **barisan belakang** saya selamat?\n- Bolehkah mereka memberi **sah** yang memenangi sesuatu?\n\n> 🧠 Dalam My Ihsan anda meletakkan buah dengan mengetik petak sasaran. Jadikan semakan kesilapan satu tabiat sebelum ketikan terakhir itu.\n\n---\n\n### Menilai kedudukan seperti master\n\nApabila tiada taktik, master menilai kedudukan menggunakan faktor ini:\n\n| Faktor | Soalan untuk ditanya |\n|---|---|\n| **Bahan** | Siapa lebih banyak? Bidak = 1, kuda = 3, gajah = 3, tir = 5, menteri = 9. |\n| **Keselamatan raja** | Adakah mana-mana raja terdedah, belum beristana atau kurang pembela? |\n| **Keaktifan buah** | Buah mana yang tidak berbuat apa-apa? Pihak mana lebih banyak buah aktif? |\n| **Struktur bidak** | Bidak terpencil, berganda atau tertinggal? Bidak lepas? |\n| **Ruang** | Siapa mengawal lebih banyak petak di tengah dan di separuh papan lawan? |\n| **Petak lemah** | Lubang yang tidak boleh dipertahankan bidak, tempat sempurna untuk kuda. |\n\nKemudian buat **rancangan**: cari buah terburuk anda dan perbaikinya, atau serang di tempat anda lebih kuat.\n\n---\n\n### Tabiat profesional\n\n1. **Berfikir dalam rancangan, bukan langkah tunggal.** \"Saya akan letak kuda di d5, kemudian tolak f4\" lebih baik daripada mencari ancaman rawak.\n2. **Pencegahan (prophylaxis).** Tanya \"apa yang lawan mahu buat?\" dan halangnya sebelum berlaku. Inilah ciri juara seperti Petrosian dan Karpov.\n3. **Tukar buah apabila mendahului, elak tukar apabila ketinggalan.** Dengan bahan lebih, permudahkan ke penamat yang senang. Apabila menyerang, kekalkan buah di papan.\n4. **Setiap buah ada tugas.** Jika sesuatu buah tiada tugas, carikan untuknya.\n5. **Gunakan jam dengan baik.** Luangkan masa pada saat genting (apabila kedudukan berubah, selepas tangkapan, apabila banyak pilihan) dan main cepat dalam kedudukan mudah.\n6. **Bertenang selepas kesilapan.** Banyak permainan dimenangi pemain yang membuat kesilapan kedua terakhir. Teruskan berjuang.\n7. **Hormati lawan.** Main setiap langkah seolah-olah mereka akan menjumpai balasan terbaik.\n\n---\n\n### Cara master belajar\n\n| Kaedah | Caranya |\n|---|---|\n| **Teka-teki taktik setiap hari** | 10 hingga 20 minit sehari. Mengenal corak ialah cara terpantas untuk maju. |\n| **Penamat** | Pelajari mat asas dan penamat raja-dan-bidak awal-awal (lihat bab 4). |\n| **Semak permainan sendiri** | Selepas setiap permainan, cari langkah anda tersilap, tanpa enjin dahulu. |\n| **Mainkan semula permainan master** | Teka setiap langkah sebelum melihat jawapan. |\n| **Main permainan lebih panjang** | Permainan perlahan (15+10 atau 30+0) mengajar lebih banyak daripada yang pantas. |\n\n#### Permainan terkenal untuk dikaji: Permainan Opera\n\nPaul Morphy mengalahkan dua bangsawan amatur di opera Paris pada 1858. Ia menunjukkan perkembangan pantas, garisan terbuka dan penamat yang indah. Mainkannya di papan sebenar atau dalam mod 2 pemain.\n\n```\n1.e4 e5        2.Nf3 d6       3.d4 Bg4\n4.dxe5 Bxf3    5.Qxf3 dxe5    6.Bc4 Nf6\n7.Qb3 Qe7      8.Nc3 c6       9.Bg5 b5\n10.Nxb5 cxb5   11.Bxb5+ Nbd7  12.O-O-O Rd8\n13.Rxd7 Rxd7   14.Rd1 Qe6     15.Bxd7+ Nxd7\n16.Qb8+ Nxb8   17.Rd8#\n```\n\n**Perkara untuk diperhatikan:**\n\n- `3...Bg4` dan `4...Bxf3` hitam memberi gajah untuk kuda dan membuang masa.\n- Menjelang langkah 12 setiap buah putih telah berkembang dan raja sudah beristana. Buah hitam di f8 dan h8 tidak pernah bergerak.\n- Putih memberi bahan (kuda, kemudian gajah, kemudian menteri) untuk mengekalkan serangan. Perkembangan lebih bernilai daripada bahan apabila raja lawan di tengah.\n- Mat terakhir hanya menggunakan tir dan gajah.\n\n---\n\n### Kesilapan biasa pemula dan penyelesaian pro\n\n| Kesilapan | Penyelesaian pro |\n|---|---|\n| Menggerakkan buah yang sama berkali-kali dalam pembukaan | Kembangkan buah baharu setiap langkah |\n| Mengeluarkan menteri terlalu awal | Kembangkan kuda dan gajah dahulu |\n| Tidak beristana | Beristana dalam 10 langkah pertama |\n| Merampas setiap bidak | Tanya kenapa ia percuma; perkembangan mungkin lebih bernilai |\n| Hanya melihat rancangan sendiri | Sentiasa semak langkah terakhir lawan dahulu |\n| Menyerang dengan satu buah | Bawa lebih banyak buah sebelum menyerang |\n| Berputus asa apabila ketinggalan | Cari helah, idea buntu dan kesilapan lawan |\n| Bermain terlalu cepat | Guna semakan kesilapan sebelum setiap langkah |\n\n---\n\n## Bab 4: Strategi dan taktik {#chapter-4}\n\n> *\"Taktik ialah tahu apa yang perlu dibuat apabila ada sesuatu untuk dibuat. Strategi ialah tahu apa yang perlu dibuat apabila tiada apa-apa untuk dibuat.\"* (dikaitkan dengan Savielly Tartakower)\n\nPermainan ada tiga fasa: **pembukaan** (lebih kurang 10 hingga 15 langkah pertama), **pertengahan** dan **penamat**. Setiap satu memerlukan cara berfikir yang berbeza.\n\n---\n\n### Bahagian A: Prinsip pembukaan\n\n1. **Kuasai tengah** (d4, d5, e4, e5) dengan bidak dan buah.\n2. **Kembangkan kuda dan gajah** dengan cepat, biasanya kuda sebelum gajah.\n3. **Beristana awal** supaya raja selamat dan tir sampai ke tengah.\n4. **Jangan gerakkan buah dua kali** tanpa sebab yang baik.\n5. **Jangan keluarkan menteri terlalu awal.** Ia akan dihalau dan anda kehilangan masa.\n6. **Sambungkan tir** (tiada apa-apa di antara keduanya di barisan belakang).\n7. **Jangan rampas bidak** dengan mengorbankan perkembangan.\n\n---\n\n### Bahagian B: Taktik, corak yang memenangi bahan\n\nKebanyakan permainan antara pemula dan pemain kelab ditentukan oleh taktik. Belajar mengenal corak ini.\n\n| Corak | Maksudnya | Contoh |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Serampang (fork)** | Satu buah menyerang dua atau lebih buah serentak | Kuda memberi sah kepada raja dan menyerang menteri |\n| **Pakuan (pin)** | Buah tidak boleh bergerak tanpa mendedahkan buah lebih bernilai di belakangnya | Gajah memaku kuda kepada raja |\n| **Sate (skewer)** | Seperti pakuan, tetapi buah lebih bernilai di depan dan mesti bergerak | Tir memberi sah kepada raja, dan menteri di belakangnya jatuh |\n| **Serangan tersingkap** | Menggerakkan satu buah membuka garisan untuk buah lain menyerang | Menggerakkan kuda mendedahkan sah gajah |\n| **Sah berganda** | Dua buah memberi sah serentak; raja mesti bergerak | Sering membawa kepada mat |\n| **Membuang pembela** | Tangkap atau halau buah yang mengawal sesuatu | Tangkap kuda yang mempertahankan barisan belakang |\n| **Pesongan (deflection)** | Paksa pembela meninggalkan tugasnya | Korban yang menarik menteri dari petak penting |\n| **Umpan (decoy)** | Pikat buah ke petak yang buruk | Korban untuk menarik raja ke dalam sah |\n| **Beban berlebihan** | Satu buah mempertahankan dua perkara; serang kedua-duanya | Ia hanya boleh menyelamatkan satu |\n| **Zwischenzug** | Langkah \"di tengah-tengah\", sering sah, sebelum tangkapan semula yang dijangka | Sah dahulu, kemudian tangkap semula |\n\n**Tip latihan:** kuda ialah buah serampang terbaik kerana ia melompat. Setiap kali kuda boleh sampai ke petak yang menyerang dua buah bernilai, semaklah.\n\n---\n\n### Bahagian C: Corak mat\n\nKenali bentuk penamat ini dan anda akan menjumpai mat dengan lebih cepat.\n\n#### Mat barisan belakang\n\nRaja terperangkap di belakang bidaknya sendiri (contohnya di g8 dengan bidak di f7, g7, h7), dan tir atau menteri mat di barisan belakang.\n\n**Pertahanan:** buat \"luft\" (petak lari) dengan menggerakkan bidak seperti `h6` / `h3`, atau simpan tir di barisan belakang.\n\n#### Mat lemas\n\nKuda memberi sah dan raja tidak boleh bergerak kerana buahnya sendiri mengelilinginya. Dilihat dalam perangkap Blackburne, Caro-Kann dan Kieninger dalam [bab 1](#chapter-1).\n\n#### Mat Arab\n\nTir dan kuda mat raja di sudut: tir memberi sah di sebelah raja (contohnya di h7 menentang raja di h8), dilindungi oleh kuda, yang juga mengawal g8. Antara corak tertua yang diketahui, sudah diterangkan dalam manuskrip shatranj berbahasa Arab.\n\n#### Mat Anastasia\n\nKuda di e7 mengawal g8 dan g6, dan tir atau menteri mat di fail h menentang raja di h7, yang laluannya disekat oleh bidaknya sendiri di g7.\n\n#### Mat Boden\n\nDua gajah pada pepenjuru bersilang mat raja yang beristana di sebelah menteri, sering selepas korban menteri di c6 atau c3.\n\n#### Mat tangga (lawnmower)\n\nDua tir (atau tir dan menteri) bergilir menolak raja ke tepi, satu baris demi satu baris, kemudian mat di situ. Cara paling mudah untuk menamatkan permainan apabila anda jauh mendahului.\n\n#### Mat menteri dan raja\n\nMenteri memberi mat di petak sebelah raja lawan, dilindungi oleh rajanya sendiri atau buah lain.\n\n---\n\n### Bahagian D: Strategi pertengahan\n\nApabila tiada taktik, ikut rancangan.\n\n#### 1. Cari buah terburuk anda dan perbaikinya\n\nGajah yang tersekat oleh bidak sendiri, kuda di tepi (\"kuda di tepi papan, lemah tenaganya\"), tir di fail tertutup: gerakkan ke petak yang lebih baik.\n\n#### 2. Tir patut berada di fail terbuka\n\n**Fail terbuka** tiada bidak. **Fail separa terbuka** hanya ada bidak lawan. Letakkan tir di situ, dan gandakan jika boleh. Tir di baris ke-7 (ke-2 untuk hitam) sangat kuat.\n\n#### 3. Kuda suka pos hadapan (outpost)\n\n**Pos hadapan** ialah petak di separuh papan lawan yang tidak boleh diserang bidak lawan, sebaiknya disokong bidak sendiri. Kuda di d5 atau e5 yang tidak boleh dihalau sering lebih bernilai daripada gajah.\n\n#### 4. Gajah suka kedudukan terbuka\n\nDua gajah (\"pasangan gajah\") kuat dalam kedudukan terbuka dengan sedikit bidak. Dalam kedudukan tertutup, kuda selalunya lebih baik.\n\n#### 5. Struktur bidak\n\n| Kelemahan | Maksudnya | Cara menggunakannya |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Bidak terpencil** | Tiada bidak kawan di fail sebelahnya | Halang dengan buah, kemudian serangnya |\n| **Bidak berganda** | Dua bidak di fail yang sama | Kurang baik saling mempertahankan |\n| **Bidak tertinggal** | Tidak boleh dilindungi jiran dan tidak boleh maju dengan selamat | Tekan bidak itu dan petak di depannya |\n| **Bidak lepas** | Tiada bidak lawan boleh menghalangnya untuk naik pangkat | \"Bidak lepas mesti ditolak.\" Sokong dengan buah |\n\n#### 6. Serang di tempat anda lebih kuat\n\nBidak menunjukkan arah. Jika rantai bidak anda menghala ke sebelah raja (contohnya bidak putih di d4 dan e5), serang di sebelah raja. Serang raja hanya apabila anda ada lebih banyak buah berhampirannya berbanding pembela.\n\n#### 7. Pecahan bidak\n\n**Pecahan bidak** ialah langkah bidak yang mencabar rantai lawan dan membuka garisan (contohnya `...c5` atau `...f6` dalam French, `...f5` dalam King's Indian). Kebanyakan rancangan dibina sekitar satu pecahan utama.\n\n#### 8. Ruang\n\nJika anda ada lebih ruang, elak pertukaran supaya lawan kekal sempit. Jika anda sempit, tukar buah untuk mendapat ruang.\n\n---\n\n### Bahagian E: Penamat\n\nDalam penamat, **raja menjadi buah pejuang**. Bawa ke tengah.\n\n#### Mat yang wajib tahu\n\n**Raja dan menteri lawan raja.** Gunakan menteri untuk mengecilkan kotak raja lawan, satu lompatan kuda darinya, bawa raja anda dekat, kemudian mat di tepi. Berhati-hati: sentiasa tinggalkan raja lawan sekurang-kurangnya satu langkah, atau ia **buntu** (seri).\n\n**Raja dan tir lawan raja.** Gunakan tir untuk memotong raja baris demi baris. Bawa raja anda bertentangan dengan raja lawan (dengan satu petak di antaranya), kemudian beri sah dengan tir di sepanjang tepi.\n\n**Dua tir lawan raja.** Mat tangga dari Bahagian C.\n\n#### Penamat raja dan bidak\n\n- **Oposisi:** apabila raja-raja bertentangan dengan satu petak di antaranya, pihak yang *tidak* perlu bergerak biasanya menang perebutan itu. Ini menentukan banyak penamat bidak.\n- **Peraturan petak:** lukis petak dari bidak ke baris naik pangkatnya. Jika raja yang bertahan boleh melangkah masuk ke petak itu, ia dapat menangkap bidak.\n- **Raja di depan bidak:** apabila menaikkan pangkat bidak dengan bantuan raja, letakkan raja di depan bidak, bukan di belakang.\n\n#### Penamat tir\n\nPenamat paling biasa. Dua idea terkenal:\n\n- **Kedudukan Lucena (menang):** bina \"jambatan\" dengan tir untuk melindungi raja daripada sah dan naikkan pangkat bidak.\n- **Kedudukan Philidor (seri):** pembela menyimpan tir di baris ketiganya sehingga bidak maju, kemudian memberi sah dari belakang.\n- Dan satu peraturan: **tir patut berada di belakang bidak lepas**, sama ada milik anda atau lawan.\n\n#### Peraturan seri yang diketahui permainan\n\nEnjin My Ihsan menggunakan peraturan penuh, jadi ingat perkara ini apabila ketinggalan:\n\n- **Buntu**: tiada langkah sah dan tidak dalam sah. Seri.\n- **Ulangan tiga kali**: kedudukan sama tiga kali. Seri.\n- **Peraturan 50 langkah**: 50 langkah setiap pihak tanpa tangkapan dan tanpa langkah bidak. Seri.\n- **Bahan tidak cukup**: contohnya raja dan gajah lawan raja. Seri.\n\nApabila kalah, cari helah buntu atau ulangan. Apabila menang, elakkannya.\n\n---\n\n### Helaian ringkas strategi\n\n| Fasa | Matlamat utama | Soalan utama |\n|---|---|---|\n| Pembukaan | Kembang, tengah, beristana | \"Sudahkah semua buah saya berkembang?\" |\n| Pertengahan | Taktik dan rancangan | \"Apa buah terburuk saya? Di mana patut saya serang?\" |\n| Penamat | Raja aktif, bidak lepas | \"Bolehkah saya buat bidak lepas? Adakah raja saya aktif?\" |\n\n---\n\n## Bab 5: Pelan latihan dalam My Ihsan {#chapter-5}\n\nCara menggunakan ciri **Teroka › Catur 3D** untuk maju langkah demi langkah.\n\n### Alat yang anda ada\n\n| Ciri | Cara ia membantu latihan |\n|---|---|\n| 🤖 **Robot tahap 1 · Mudah** | Latih prinsip pembukaan dan perangkap cepat dari bab 1 |\n| 🤖 **Robot tahap 2 · Sederhana** | Latih pembukaan dan taktik asas |\n| 🤖 **Robot tahap 3 · Sukar** | Permainan sebenar: main perlahan dan guna rutin berfikir |\n| ⚪ / ⚫ / 🔀 **Saya main** | Latih kedua-dua warna; pembukaan hitam juga perlu dilatih |\n| ⏱ **Jam** (Tiada, 3+2, 5+0, 10+0, 15+10, 30+0) | Mula tanpa jam atau 30+0; guna 3+2 dan 5+0 hanya untuk suka-suka |\n| ↶ **Undur** | Tarik balik kesilapan dan cari langkah betul (permainan latihan sahaja) |\n| 🔄 **Pusing papan** | Lihat kedudukan dari sisi lawan untuk mengesan ancaman mereka |\n| 📜 **Senarai langkah** | Semak permainan dan cari titik perubahannya |\n| 👥 **2 pemain** | Bermain dengan kawan dan keluarga, atau mainkan semula permainan terkenal |\n| ↩ **Sambung** | Permainan disimpan, jadi anda boleh memikirkan kedudukan sukar kemudian |\n| 📖 **Panduan** | Setiap baris dalam panduan ini boleh dimainkan di papan, dan diteruskan dalam 3D |\n\n---\n\n### Minggu 1: Asas dan perangkap cepat\n\n- **Pelajari notasi** dari [awal panduan](#chapter-0). Perhatikan senarai 📜 langkah semasa bermain.\n- Mainkan **Fool's mate dan Scholar's mate** dalam mod 2 pemain untuk melihat cara ia berlaku, kemudian mainkan pertahanan untuk setiap satu.\n- Main **5 permainan menentang robot tahap 1** sebagai putih, menggunakan Italian Game. Matlamat: beristana dalam setiap permainan.\n- Pelajari mat **raja dan menteri lawan raja**. Latih sehingga anda boleh melakukannya tanpa buntu.\n\n### Minggu 2: Pembukaan dan taktik\n\n- Pilih **satu pembukaan sebagai putih** dan **satu balasan kepada 1.e4** dari [bab 2](#chapter-2).\n- Main **5 permainan menentang tahap 2** dengan setiap warna.\n- Setiap hari, luangkan 10 minit untuk corak taktik dalam [bab 4](#chapter-4): serampang, pakuan dan sate.\n- Selepas setiap permainan, buka senarai langkah dan catat langkah pertama anda kehilangan bahan.\n\n### Minggu 3: Rutin berfikir\n\n- Main **tahap 2 dengan jam 30+0**. Sebelum setiap langkah, ikut rutin dalam [bab 3](#chapter-3): langkah terakhir lawan → sah, tangkapan, ancaman → calon → semakan kesilapan.\n- Guna **Undur** hanya selepas permainan tamat: kembali ke kesilapan dan cari langkah yang lebih baik.\n- Pelajari mat **raja dan tir lawan raja** dan **peraturan petak**.\n- Mainkan semula **Permainan Opera** dalam mod 2 pemain, sambil meneka langkah putih.\n\n### Minggu 4: Permainan sebenar\n\n- Main **tahap 3 dengan 15+10**. Tiada undur semasa permainan.\n- Tambah **satu balasan kepada 1.d4** dalam repertoir anda.\n- Semak setiap kekalahan: adakah ia taktik yang terlepas (bab 4, Bahagian B), rancangan buruk (Bahagian D) atau jam?\n- Matlamat: kemenangan pertama menentang tahap 3.\n\n---\n\n### Rutin harian 20 minit\n\n| Minit | Aktiviti |\n|---|---|\n| 5 | Ulang kaji satu perangkap atau corak mat dari bab 1 dan 4 |\n| 10 | Satu permainan menentang robot (atau beberapa langkah permainan panjang yang disimpan) |\n| 5 | Lihat senarai langkah dan cari langkah terbaik dan terburuk anda |\n\n---\n\n### Senarai semak sebelum setiap langkah ✅\n\n1. Apa yang baru dibuat oleh lawan?\n2. Ada sah, tangkapan atau ancaman, untuk saya atau mereka?\n3. Adakah saya ada 2 atau 3 langkah calon?\n4. Apa balasan terbaik mereka?\n5. Adakah apa-apa milik saya tidak dipertahankan? Adakah barisan belakang saya selamat?\n\nSemoga berjaya, dan selamat bermain. ♟️\n"};
